\section{Exposure is not always the x-axis variable}{

\code{plot_by} (\code{object$group}), not \code{exposure}, drives a VPC's x-axis; the two
only coincide when the caller didn't override \code{plot_by}. A builder that
needs the x-axis variable's own label or numeric range should read
\code{object$group$label}/\code{config$group_limits} instead of \code{exposure$label}/
\code{exposure$limits} -- see \code{\link[=er_vpc]{er_vpc()}}'s \code{plot_by} argument.
}

\section{Writing your own builder}{


Every \verb{er_style_vpc_*()} function above shares the signature documented
in the "Arguments" section above, and that signature is a public part of
the API: any function \verb{function(data, config, exposure, response, theme, ...)} that returns a geom or list of geoms can stand in for a built-in
builder, passed as \code{style} to \code{\link[=er_vpc_add_observed]{er_vpc_add_observed()}}/
\code{\link[=er_vpc_add_simulated]{er_vpc_add_simulated()}}.

A custom builder can self-declare metadata via \code{\link[=er_style_tag]{er_style_tag()}}:
\code{vpc_layout} (\code{"categorical"}/\code{"continuous"}, checked for agreement
between the observed and simulated builders paired on one \code{er_vpc}
object -- distinct from the data layer's own \code{layout} tag, which uses
an unrelated \code{"overlay"}/\code{"panel"} pair), \code{layer} (\code{"observed"}/
\code{"simulated"}, checked against the layer the builder was actually
passed to), \code{response_types}/\code{plot_by_types} (checked against
\code{object$response$type}/\code{object$group$type}), and \code{marker_source}
(which of \code{config$summary}/\code{config$percentiles} the builder actually
draws from, used by \code{\link[=er_vpc_theme]{er_vpc_theme()}}'s \code{xlim}/\code{ylim} cropping). All
five are optional and independent -- see \code{\link[=er_style_tag]{er_style_tag()}} for the full
explanation of each, including which built-in builders use them and
why.

Both \code{observed} and \code{simulated} are \strong{singleton} layers: calling
\code{\link[=er_vpc_add_observed]{er_vpc_add_observed()}}/\code{\link[=er_vpc_add_simulated]{er_vpc_add_simulated()}} again replaces the
previous builder rather than adding another one.
}

\section{Passing extra arguments to a builder}{


\code{\link[=er_vpc_add_observed]{er_vpc_add_observed()}}/\code{\link[=er_vpc_add_simulated]{er_vpc_add_simulated()}} each take their own
\code{...}, forwarded unchanged to \code{style} when it's actually called at build
time. Extra arguments must be named, since they're appended positionally
after the five standard arguments; an unnamed one errors immediately
rather than silently binding to the wrong parameter. A builder that
doesn't need any extra arguments simply declares \code{...} and ignores it --
every built-in VPC builder does exactly this.
}

